  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Homogeneity",
    "lesson_core_move": "Verify homogeneity by testing whether a continuous-time system that maps $x(t)$ to $y(t)$ also maps any scaled input a $x(t)$ to the scaled output a $y(t)$ as part of linearity.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Homogeneity",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Homogeneity",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A continuous-time system is homogeneous when scaling the input by a real factor produces the same scale factor at the output. This is the linearity test you use after you can write the system as $T\\{x(t)\\}=y(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x(t)\\}=y(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The homogeneity check is $T\\{a x(t)\\}=a T\\{x(t)\\}=a y(t)$, with $a\\in\\mathbb{R}$. The system rule stays the same; only the input and output scale together by the same factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{a x(t)\\}=a T\\{x(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The simplest mental test is to start with one input-output pair $x(t)\\mapsto y(t)$ and check what happens when the input is doubled or negated. If doubling gives $2y(t)$ and negating gives $-y(t)$, the system passes this test; if either one fails, the system is not homogeneous and therefore not linear.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Homogeneity",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Comparing the Output for a Scaled Input",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A system maps $x(t)$ to $y(t)$. If the input is changed to $3x(t)$, what output should a homogeneous system produce?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the known input-output pair $x(t) \\mapsto y(t)$. The homogeneity test asks whether multiplying the input by a real constant multiplies the output by the same constant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x(t)\\}=y(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now replace the input with $3x(t)$. For a homogeneous system, the output should keep the same scale factor, so the new output is $3y(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{3x(t)\\}=3T\\{x(t)\\}=3y(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the input was scaled by $3$ and the output was also scaled by $3$, this example passes the homogeneity check. The same comparison works the same way for any real scale factor $a$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Homogeneity",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Verifying Homogeneity When Scaling Passes Through the System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A system is defined by $y(t)=4x(t)$. Verify homogeneity for the input $a x(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a linear gain rule: the output is already written as a constant times the input, so the homogeneity test is to replace $x(t)$ with $a x(t)$ and see whether the same factor $a$ can be carried through the system.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x(t)\\}=4x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the scaled input into the rule. The system output becomes $T\\{a x(t)\\}=4[a x(t)]$, and the scalar factor can be moved outside the system action without changing the output shape.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{a x(t)\\}=4a x(t)=a\\bigl(4x(t)\\bigr)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $4x(t)=y(t)$, the result is $T\\{a x(t)\\}=a y(t)$. The input scale and the output scale match exactly, so this system is homogeneous.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Homogeneity",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Identifying a Homogeneity Failure from a Mismatched Scale Factor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A system is defined by $y(t)=x^2(t)$. Test homogeneity using the input $a x(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a nonlinear square rule: the output is the square of the input, so test homogeneity by replacing $x(t)$ with $a x(t)$ and checking whether the output keeps the same scale factor $a$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x^2(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the scaled input. Squaring $a x(t)$ gives $a^2 x^2(t)$, so the scale factor moves from $a$ to $a^2$ instead of staying the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{a x(t)\\}=(a x(t))^2=a^2x^2(t)=a^2y(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compare that result with $a y(t)$. Because the output scales as $a^2 y(t)$ rather than $a y(t)$, the system fails the homogeneity test.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Homogeneity",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Testing Homogeneity with an Arbitrary Scale Constant",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A system is defined by $y(t)=7x(t)$. Show that for any scalar $a$, the input $a x(t)$ produces output $a y(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the gain rule $y(t)=7x(t)$. To test homogeneity, replace $x(t)$ with $a x(t)$ and track the same scale factor through the system.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=7x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the scaled input: $T\\{a x(t)\\}=7(a x(t))=7a x(t)$. Because scalar multiplication can be regrouped, rewrite this as $a(7x(t))$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{a x(t)\\}=7(a x(t))=7a x(t)=a(7x(t))=a y(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $7x(t)=y(t)$, the output is exactly $a y(t)$. The same scalar appears on the output, so this system satisfies homogeneity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
